  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/ab95faf73fa837d232a67a773afdbf456f5798d0f05de23e6ce44b41cddb1881.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta $ (shown above) given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{\u2032}(\\theta )=-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\theta ).\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(\\theta )}{{x}^{\u2032}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\theta )}{-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
